# The build context for THE image — ./Dockerfile, built with `context: .` (this directory, the reactor
# root) because `orchestrator` resolves `tech.mikhailov.fsm:engine` AND `tech.mikhailov.fsm:runner`
# from the reactor. That build COPYs named poms and named src/ trees, so nothing here changes what ends
# up in an image; what it changes is how much of this worktree is shipped to the daemon first. Without
# it that is ~130 MB of build output, differential-harness fixtures and node_modules per build, which is
# most of the wall clock of an otherwise cached build.
#
# THIS IS THE ONLY .dockerignore THE STACK USES, as of 2026-08-06. There was a second one beside
# `engine/Dockerfile`, which was built with `context: ../engine` and so did not consult this file; both
# went when the engine profile stopped being a second image and became `fsm:latest` plus a
# PropertiesLauncher entrypoint. Its sharper wording of the first rule below is kept here rather than
# lost: a copied `target/` would let a stale jar, compiled by whatever JDK the developer happens to
# have, win over the one the image builds from source.
**/target/

# Differential-harness OUTPUT: the unpacked copies of the frozen corpora, tens of megabytes, rebuilt by
# `node harness/unpack-fixtures.cjs`. Nothing reads them but a person.
#
# NOT harness/fixtures/ — those are the frozen JavaScript answers themselves, they are committed, and
# the harness TESTS read them during `mvn package` inside the image build. Excluding them here would
# fail every image build with a missing-fixture error, which is the one failure mode a .dockerignore is
# worst at explaining. harness/README.md is committed and is not output either. harness/report.txt is
# NOT committed — it is generated into target/harness/ by the test and gitignored.
**/harness/out/

# Node dependencies and stale Python bytecode. The n8n generator tree that these also covered
# (n8n/agentic/coverage/, n8n/agentic/reports/) is deleted; the two rules went with it rather than
# being left as paths that match nothing. What remains is named narrowly for a reason: a wildcard as
# broad as `**/coverage/` would one day match a directory inside a module's src/ tree and silently
# drop it from an image, which is the failure mode a .dockerignore is worst at showing you.
**/node_modules/
**/__pycache__/

# Live state, not source: n8n's sqlite database and Data Tables, and the orchestrator's default-path H2
# store from a `mvn spring-boot:run` in that directory — and, since the prover moved into that process,
# its default-path checkout cache as well: fsm.runner.cache defaults to ./data/cache, so a local run
# leaves a couple of dozen cloned third-party repositories under orchestrator/data/. Those are hundreds
# of megabytes that must never reach a build context. (The directory is `deploy/` now; `n8n-data` is
# the basename n8n itself used, so it stays.)
deploy/n8n-data/
orchestrator/data/
**/*.mv.db
**/*.trace.db
**/*.lock.db

# Never part of a build, and .git in particular carries the full history into every context.
.git/
**/.idea/
**/*.iml
